    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d361">

                <pb facs="0376" n="364" xml:id="pg364"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">760C.62/1007: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Poland</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Biddle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Warsaw</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-08-25T15:00:00+01:00">August 25, 1939—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 25—1:58 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>205. 1. At 1:50 during a conversation with Beck, official telephone operator
                    informed him all telephone lines from Warsaw to Paris through Germany and from
                    Warsaw to Rome were cut. Beck admitted serious implications thereof and added
                    that Poland was ready for any eventuality.</p>
                <p>2. He informed me President Moscicki was profoundly appreciative of President
                    Roosevelt’s constructive and humanitarian interest as evidenced by his cable to
                    which President Moscicki would immediately cable response, contents of which he
                    would subsequently impart to me.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Biddle</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
